      "problem": "$\\{1,\\sqrt{2},\\pi \\}⊔\\{0,\\sqrt{3},2\\pi \\}=$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the sets are disjoint since\n\n$$\n\\{1,\\sqrt{2},\\pi \\}\\cap \\{0,\\sqrt{3},2\\pi \\}=∅.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the disjoint union is simply the union:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{1,\\sqrt{2},\\pi \\}⊔\\{0,\\sqrt{3},2\\pi \\} & =\\{1,\\sqrt{2},\\pi \\}\\cup \\{0,\\sqrt{3},2\\pi \\} \\\\  & =\\{0,1,\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{3},\\pi ,2\\pi \\}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
